MASTER UPGRADE PROMPT: COMPLETE UNIVERSITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM — ALL ROLES, RESPONSIBILITIES, ADMIN CONTROL, AND OPEN USER REGISTRATION

Act as a senior university ERP architect, full-stack software engineer, database designer, academic systems specialist, UI/UX designer, and application security expert.

Upgrade the existing School Management System into a complete, modern, professional University Management System (UMS) suitable for universities and other higher institutions in Nigeria.

The application must support undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, faculties, departments, academic sessions, semesters, course registration, examinations, results, CGPA, transcripts, admissions, finance, staff, library, hostel, e-learning, student affairs, and university administration.

IMPORTANT: Preserve all working features from the existing project. Extend and improve the software without unnecessarily deleting, breaking, or replacing existing functionality.

1. AUTHENTICATION AND REGISTRATION RULES

Implement the following exact authentication policy.

Admin login

- The Admin must have a dedicated login page.
- Admin accounts must be created securely through a protected initial setup process or secure database seeding.
- Do not provide unrestricted public Admin registration.
- Admin must log in with authorized credentials.
- Admin must have full system-management authority across the entire university software.
- Require strong password policies and secure session management.
- Support optional two-factor authentication.
- Provide secure password reset and account recovery.

Registration for every other role

Create a public registration page for all non-admin user categories.

Registration options must include:

- Student
- Lecturer / Academic Staff
- Non-Academic Staff
- Parent / Guardian
- Applicant
- Other approved university user categories

If a user already has an account, display a clear “Already have an account? Login” link.

If the user does not have an account, display a “Create Account / Register” option.

During registration:

- Ask for the information required for the selected user category.
- Validate email, phone number, password, and other required fields.
- Prevent duplicate accounts.
- Verify email or phone when a verification provider is configured.
- Save the registration securely.
- Display registration status and next steps.
- Create the correct user profile and role association.
- Log registration activity.

IMPORTANT SECURITY RULE: Public registration must never allow a person to become Admin by choosing an Admin role. New registrations must not receive privileged permissions automatically.

Role activation and approval

- Students may create an account, but access to official university records must depend on verified admission or an approved student record.
- Applicants may register and submit admission applications.
- Lecturers and staff may register, but privileged staff access must require approval by the Admin or an authorized office.
- Parents must be linked to the correct student through a verified relationship or an authorized university process.
- Users must not be able to select arbitrary student IDs, staff IDs, departments, or permissions to gain access.
- Admin can approve, reject, suspend, reactivate, or deactivate user accounts.

Login routing

After login, route each user to the correct dashboard based on their assigned role and permissions.

A student must never be routed to the lecturer or Admin dashboard unless separately authorized.

2. ADMIN — FULL SYSTEM RESPONSIBILITY

Admin is the highest-authority application user.

Give Admin a dedicated, comprehensive dashboard and access to every module in the university software.

Admin must be able to:

A. University configuration

- Set university name, logo, motto, address, contact details, website, academic calendar, timezone, currency, and official branding.
- Configure academic sessions, semesters, terms, grading systems, assessment rules, and institutional policies.
- Configure faculties, departments, schools, centres, institutes, campuses, and programmes.
- Configure admission rules, student status categories, graduation requirements, and registration deadlines.

B. Users and permissions

- View all users and their account statuses.
- Approve or reject registrations and staff access requests.
- Create, edit, suspend, reactivate, and deactivate user accounts.
- Assign roles and permissions.
- Reset accounts securely and initiate password recovery.
- Review login history and account activity.
- Enforce least-privilege access and revoke inappropriate permissions.
- Never display existing users' plaintext passwords.

C. Complete academic administration

- Create and manage faculties and departments.
- Configure programmes, degree levels, course codes, credit units, prerequisites, and curricula.
- Manage student admissions and matriculation.
- Manage student enrolment, course registration, add/drop deadlines, and approval workflows.
- Configure examinations, assessment weights, grading scales, GPA and CGPA calculation rules.
- Review and authorize result publication workflows.
- Manage graduation eligibility, clearance, and transcript requests.

D. Finance and payments

- Configure tuition and other charges.
- Review invoices, payments, scholarships, waivers, refunds, and outstanding balances.
- Review financial reports and audit logs.
- Configure official payment gateway integrations.
- Approve sensitive financial operations according to configured policies.
- Prevent unauthorized changes to verified transactions.

E. Staff and operations

- Manage academic and non-academic staff.
- Manage faculties, departments, job titles, staff assignments, and institutional responsibilities.
- Configure payroll records if the payroll module is enabled.
- Manage library, hostel, transport, inventory, procurement, and maintenance modules.
- Manage university-wide announcements, notifications, events, and calendars.

F. Reports and analytics

- View university-wide statistics.
- Generate academic, admissions, financial, attendance, staffing, and operational reports.
- Export permitted reports to PDF, CSV, and Excel where supported.
- Review audit logs and important changes.
- View module activity and system health indicators.

G. Platform maintenance

- Manage application settings, integrations, feature toggles, backups, and maintenance notices.
- Review errors and failed background jobs.
- Configure supported email, SMS, storage, and payment providers.
- Manage database backup and recovery procedures.
- Monitor security events and suspicious activity.

Admin authority safeguards

Admin has full application-management authority, but sensitive operations must remain auditable.

- Record who performed each sensitive action and when.
- Require confirmation for destructive actions.
- Prefer archive or soft-delete for important academic and financial records.
- Do not allow permanent deletion of financial transactions or published academic history without a legally and institutionally appropriate correction process.
- Never expose passwords, private API keys, or payment secrets to dashboard users.
- Keep database backup and recovery procedures separate from ordinary user actions.

3. COMPLETE RESPONSIBILITIES FOR EVERY ROLE

Implement the following role responsibilities. All permissions must be enforced on the backend, not only hidden in the user interface.

3.1 Applicant

Responsibilities:

- Register an account.
- Complete personal and educational information.
- Select preferred programme and entry route.
- Upload required application documents.
- Pay application fees through a verified payment integration if required.
- Track application status.
- Receive admission decisions and notifications.
- Accept or decline an offer.
- Download admission letters when authorized.
- Convert to an admitted student profile after the official admission process is completed.

Applicants must not access private records belonging to other applicants.

3.2 Student

Responsibilities:

- Maintain permitted personal information.
- View admission and matriculation details.
- View faculty, department, programme, level, and academic adviser.
- Register courses during authorized registration periods.
- Add or drop courses where permitted.
- View registered courses and credit units.
- View class timetable and examination timetable.
- Access assignments, learning materials, and announcements.
- View published results, semester GPA, cumulative CGPA, and academic standing.
- View invoices, payment receipts, scholarships, and outstanding balances.
- Apply for hostel accommodation, deferment, leave of absence, change of programme, or other permitted student services.
- Submit transcript, certificate, and clearance requests.
- View attendance where the university enables it.
- Track SIWES, internship, industrial training, and final-year project progress.
- Receive official notifications.
- Submit support tickets and authorized appeals.

Students must not edit official results, change their CGPA, approve their own payments, or alter their academic history.

3.3 Lecturer / Academic Staff

Responsibilities:

- Maintain an authorized staff profile.
- View assigned courses and classes.
- Access class lists for assigned courses.
- Upload lecture notes, reading materials, and announcements.
- Create assignments and configure deadlines.
- Record attendance where enabled.
- Enter continuous assessment and examination marks for assigned courses.
- Save drafts and submit results for review.
- View the status of result submissions.
- Supervise assigned projects and SIWES placements where authorized.
- Communicate with assigned students through approved channels.
- View relevant teaching timetables and departmental notices.

Lecturers may not publish or alter finalized results unless explicitly authorized by institutional policy. All score changes after submission must be logged and subject to an approved correction workflow.

3.4 Head of Department / HOD

Responsibilities:

- View department-specific academic information.
- Manage departmental course offerings within authorized limits.
- Review course assignments and departmental teaching schedules.
- Recommend lecturers for course assignments.
- Review submitted results and departmental performance.
- Verify course registration exceptions and academic progression requests.
- Review project supervision assignments.
- Review departmental graduation eligibility.
- Generate departmental reports.
- Recommend academic corrections through formal approval workflows.

An HOD must not automatically gain access to unrelated departments or other faculties.

3.5 Dean of Faculty

Responsibilities:

- Oversee authorized faculty departments.
- Review faculty-wide academic performance.
- Review departmental result approvals and exceptional cases.
- Monitor staffing and course delivery.
- Review curriculum and programme proposals.
- Review faculty reports, academic appeals, and graduation eligibility.
- Coordinate faculty meetings and announcements.
- Submit recommendations to university management.

A Dean's access must be restricted to their assigned faculty unless a higher permission is explicitly granted.

3.6 Registrar / Admissions Officer

Responsibilities:

- Manage applications and admissions workflows.
- Verify required admission documents.
- Record admission decisions through authorized procedures.
- Generate admission lists and admission letters.
- Manage matriculation numbers and student registration records.
- Maintain official student biodata.
- Process approved transfers, deferments, withdrawals, and programme changes.
- Maintain student academic status records.
- Support official transcript, certificate, and clearance requests.
- Generate admissions and registry reports.

The Registrar must not bypass the configured approval process or alter published results without authorization.

3.7 Bursar / Accountant

Responsibilities:

- Configure authorized fee schedules.
- Generate invoices and student account statements.
- Record and reconcile verified payments.
- Manage approved scholarships, waivers, and refunds.
- Track outstanding fees and arrears.
- Manage approved expenditure and budget records.
- Produce financial reports and reconciliation statements.
- Review payment gateway settlement and transaction references.
- Investigate failed, pending, or duplicated payment notifications.
- Maintain audit trails for financial operations.

Accountants must not alter examination results or gain access to unrelated confidential information.

3.8 Departmental / Faculty Administrator

Responsibilities:

- Maintain authorized departmental records.
- Assist with course allocation and timetable administration.
- Manage approved departmental documents and notices.
- Prepare departmental reports.
- Process administrative requests.
- Support course registration and result workflows within assigned permissions.

3.9 Librarian

Responsibilities:

- Manage books, journals, digital resources, and library categories.
- Register library items and track availability.
- Issue and return books.
- Manage reservations and renewals.
- Track overdue items and authorized fines.
- Manage library membership.
- Generate borrowing and inventory reports.
- Manage authorized digital resources.
- Record library clearance status for graduation or withdrawal workflows.

3.10 Hostel / Accommodation Officer

Responsibilities:

- Manage hostels, blocks, rooms, and bed spaces.
- Configure accommodation capacity and fees.
- Process accommodation applications.
- Assign rooms according to approved rules.
- Track occupancy, room changes, maintenance issues, and vacancies.
- Generate accommodation lists and reports.
- Update hostel clearance status.

3.11 Examination Officer

Responsibilities:

- Configure examination schedules.
- Allocate examination venues and permitted seating arrangements.
- Coordinate examination timetables.
- Monitor result submission deadlines.
- Review result processing status.
- Manage approved examination records and reports.
- Record authorized examination incidents and corrections.
- Support examination-board approval workflows.

3.12 ICT / Technical Support Officer

Responsibilities:

- Handle approved technical support tickets.
- Assist users with account access.
- Monitor system availability and integration status.
- Review permitted diagnostic logs.
- Help configure institutional devices and supported integrations.
- Escalate security incidents.

ICT staff must not automatically receive access to student financial details, private academic records, or unrestricted impersonation powers.

3.13 Parent / Sponsor

Responsibilities:

- Register and maintain an account.
- Request verified linkage to a student.
- View only linked students' permitted academic and financial summaries.
- View published results and attendance where authorized.
- View invoices and verified payment receipts.
- Receive announcements and relevant notifications.
- Contact authorized university offices.

Parent access must not expose confidential student records without the necessary legal authority and institutional permission.

3.14 University Management

Responsibilities:

- View authorized university-wide performance dashboards.
- Review academic, admissions, finance, staffing, and operational reports.
- Review policy proposals and strategic plans.
- Monitor institutional KPIs.
- Review approved budgets and departmental summaries.
- Access management reports within assigned permissions.

Management is a separate role from Admin. It must not receive unrestricted technical or security permissions by default.

3.15 System Support and Other Approved Roles

Support personnel and other roles must receive explicitly defined permissions. Do not give every staff account universal access.

Allow Admin to configure additional roles and individual permissions.

